course: dictionary
lesson: 12464
title: Defining words and clauses
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word defining has two main jobs. Most often it describes something very important in showing what a person or thing is really like: «Winning the race was a defining moment for Ella.» Here, defining means the moment that showed what Ella was really like.
explain: Defining can also be a verb, the -ing form of define. To define a word is to give its exact meaning, so a dictionary defines words for us.
explain: In grammar, a defining relative clause tells us exactly which person or thing is meant: «The girl who won the race is my sister.» The words who won the race tell us which girl. No commas are used, because the clause is needed to know who we mean.
fact: Defining (as an adjective) means most important in showing what someone or something is really like.
fact: «A defining moment» is an event that changes a person or a story for ever.
fact: «A defining feature» is the part of something that makes it what it is.
fact: Defining (as a verb) means giving the exact meaning of a word.
fact: A defining relative clause uses who, which or that, with no commas, to show exactly which person or thing is meant.
exercise: «Winning the gold medal was a defining moment for Ayo.» What does defining mean here? | Hard to see | *Most important in showing what Ayo is really like | Written down in a book | Very short and quick
exercise: A dictionary is used for defining words. What does defining mean here? | Drawing a picture of a word | Reading a story aloud | Counting the letters in a word | *Giving the exact meaning of a word
exercise: Find the defining relative clause: «The boy who lives next door has a dog.» | next door | *who lives next door | the boy | has a dog
test: «Her kindness was a defining feature of her character.» What does a defining feature show? | The shortest word in a sentence | *The part that makes someone what they really are | A feature that is always hidden | A part of a car that is painted red
test: Which sentence uses a defining relative clause with no commas? | The book, that you lent me, is on the desk. | Book that lent you desk. | *The book that you lent me is on the desk. | The book is on the desk, that you lent me.
exam: Which sentence uses defining to mean giving the exact meaning? | *The teacher is defining the word brave, so we know exactly what it means. | The teacher is defining the window with a pencil. | The teacher is defining a sandwich for lunch. | The teacher is defining the sky as blue for the picture.
